The committed verifier uses only Python integers and `fractions.Fraction` for proof checking. Floating-point linear programming was used only to discover candidate dual supports before rational reconstruction.

The exact verifier reports:

\[
157\text{ excluded}=93\,C_7+64\,(C_3\sqcup C_4),
\]

leaving

\[
27\,C_7.
\]

The surviving one-based orbit indices are

\[
115,118,129,130,134,135,136,137,138,139,140,141,142,143,144,145,146,147,148,169,170,171,172,173,174,175,176.
\]

Cross-checking these against the previously committed 77-orbit endpoint shows that all 27 are old survivors. Thus the new layer removes exactly

\[
50=20\,C_7+30\,(C_3\sqcup C_4)
\]

additional old survivors. In particular, it removes all 30 `C3\sqcup C4` cases that survived the prior affine-local reduction.

## 11. Attempts that do not justify stronger claims

Two stronger necessary-condition searches were also tested against the 27 survivors but did not eliminate any of them:

- a larger signed-distance negative-type cone using primitive coefficient vectors in `[-2,2]^8`;
- the necessary ground signed-force condition that no consecutive force-direction gap reaches `pi`.

These computations are not used in the theorem and are not certificates of realizability. They indicate that more of the same linear/angular filtering is unlikely to finish generic `H7b`.

The remaining useful layer is nonlinear: actual equilateral-pentagon vector closure, the seven-force ground closure, law-of-sines radius-cycle integrability, and common reciprocal-coordinate reconstruction.

## 12. Final disposition

The new `77 -> 27` generic `H7b` reduction is supported by exact finite certificates and survives the branch-choice and strictness audit. All surviving generic cases are `C7`; all generic `C3\sqcup C4` cases are excluded.

**Audit disposition: PASS within scope.**

No theorem-state file is changed. C-II remains open.
